## Application Logs

The Grafana profile provisions Alloy and Loki, a Loki datasource, and [Logs Overview](http://localhost:13001/d/goforj-logs-overview). Local apps export automatically when the profile is active. Bind `appLogger.WithContext(ctx)` to correlate logs with the active trace and span. Expand a log record to open its Tempo trace; the trace view links back to related logs.

Remote log export is independent from traces. Set `OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp` or a log-specific OTLP endpoint to enable it, or `OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER=none` to disable it. An existing generic trace endpoint does not silently authorize exporting application log contents. See `internal/logger/README.md` for configuration, field limits, queue behavior, and shutdown handling.

Loki keeps data in a separate volume, applies seven-day retention, and indexes only service name, app, and deployment environment. Trace and request identifiers remain structured metadata. Loki and Alloy are local development services; configure access controls and storage for an external deployment.

## Request Traces

The `grafana` Compose profile includes Tempo, its persistent storage, a provisioned datasource, and [Traces Overview](http://localhost:13001/d/goforj-traces-overview). With `APP_ENV=local`, generated apps export automatically when that profile is active. Start the stack with `docker-compose up -d --build`, run `forj dev`, and request an application route. Open Traces Overview, select a service, and click a trace to inspect the span waterfall. Grafana Explore also supports TraceQL queries such as `{}`.

Application spans created with standard OpenTelemetry APIs appear alongside framework spans. Lighthouse continues to show its richer local capture using the same OTel trace IDs.

Explicit `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` or `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` settings override local Tempo. Use standard OTLP endpoint and header settings for Grafana Cloud or another backend. `OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER=none` disables export; `OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true` disables recording. Automatic export is inactive in production, staging, and test processes. See `internal/inspects/README.md` for sampling and instrumentation.

The generated stack is for local development. Tempo has no built-in authentication and its published ports bind locally by default. Configure authentication, access controls, retention, and durable storage for a production deployment. Removing the Docker volume deletes its local trace history.

## Grafana

Grafana is provisioned with:

- datasources: VictoriaMetrics (metrics), Tempo (traces), Loki (logs)
- dashboards:
  - Traces Overview
  - Logs Overview
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Cache }}
  - Cache Overview
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Storage }}
  - Storage Overview
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Events }}
  - Events Overview
{{- end }}
  - Auth Overview
{{- if .Components.Mail }}
  - Mail Overview
{{- end }}
  - HTTP Overview
{{- if .Components.HasDatabase }}
  - Database Overview
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Jobs }}
  - Queue Overview
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.Scheduler }}
  - Scheduler Overview
{{- end }}
- starred dashboards: generated overviews are starred automatically for the default admin user
- home dashboard: Platform Overview

The dashboards are authored against GoForj's metric contract rather than generic third-party dashboard imports.

Metric dashboards include an App filter backed by the emitted `app` metric label. Traces Overview and Logs Overview use the OpenTelemetry service name. Primitive dashboards also include filters for named accessors when applicable.

{{- if .ProjectComponents.Cache }}
- Cache Overview can be filtered to one named cache.
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Storage }}
- Storage Overview can be filtered to one disk.
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.HasDatabase }}
- Database Overview can be filtered to one connection or table.
{{- end }}

If rendered dashboards do not refresh immediately:

1. Re-render the project with `forj render`.
2. Rebuild Grafana, then rerun the one-shot seeder explicitly:
   - `docker-compose up -d --build grafana`
   - `docker-compose run --rm --no-deps grafana-seed`
3. Wait for Grafana provisioning to rescan dashboards.
{{- end }}

## Querying

Use Grafana Explore or the VictoriaMetrics query UI to inspect raw metrics. Common production queries and the emitted metric families are documented in `internal/metrics/README.md`.

The most useful query dimensions are:

- `app`: which App composition app emitted the metric, such as `app`, `admin`, or `statuspage`.
- `source`: which logical source emitted the metric.
- `process`: which scrape target exposed the metric.
- `release`: the optional operator-facing release version attached by the scrape target.
- `revision`: the optional immutable source or artifact revision attached by the scrape target.
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Cache }}
- `cache`: which named cache handled the work.
{{- end }}
- `mailer` and `connection`: which named primitive handled the work.
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Storage }}
- `disk`: which named storage disk handled the work.
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Jobs }}
- `queue`: which named queue handled the work.
{{- end }}
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Events }}
- `topic`, `handler`, and `bus`: which event path handled the work.
{{- end }}
- `route`, `method`, `status`: which HTTP route and outcome generated traffic.
- `job_name`, `target_kind`: which named unit of background work ran.
{{- if .ProjectComponents.Events }}
- `topic`, `handler`: which event topic or subscriber handled work.
{{- end }}

## Production Notes

- The rendered stack is optimized for local development and production-like troubleshooting.
- In Kubernetes, scrape each pod/process and let the metrics backend aggregate by label.
- Keep labels bounded. Do not add user IDs, emails, cache keys, raw URLs, raw SQL, request IDs, or arbitrary error strings as labels.
{{- if .Components.Grafana }}
- Change `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` before exposing Grafana outside local development.
{{- end }}
